seen, out = set(), []
for x in xs:
    if x not in seen:
        seen.add(x)
        out.append(x)
Each element is checked against and added to a set — O(1) average per operation — so the pass is O(n) overall. list(set(xs)) is also O(n) (construction plus the later sort to restore order is O(n log n)), but sets are unordered, so the result silently reorders elements: 'first occurrence wins' information is lost. When order carries meaning — log lines, first-come ranks, positional pairing with another list — that loss is a correctness bug, not a cosmetic one.

